---
source: crates/tisty-cli/tests/mcp_catalogue.rs
expression: "rules(Some(\"documents\"))"
---
## Write it to be read

Whatever you write or edit — a task's description, a journal note, a task's page, a document — the person reads in the window, so make it visual, readable and in order, never a wall of plain text:
- A heading for each part when there is more than one, and short paragraphs, one line each. A task's page keeps the sections it has, so write under them; Brainstorm takes ideas alone, a few words each.
- A list for findings, options or what is left, numbered when the order matters; a table to compare.
- **Bold** for the one thing to see first; `code` for commands, paths and names; a link where it leads.
- A callout for what must stand out — `[!TIP]`, `[!IMPORTANT]`, `[!WARNING]` — and a pen or an icon where it helps the eye, sparingly.
- A box `- [ ]` only for a step: on a task's page it becomes one.

When you edit, leave the passage at least as ordered as you found it. `language` gives Tisty's marks one topic at a time.

## Documents

Start with `catch_up`, which says where things stand and what moved since you were last here; `brain` names the folders, and with `folder` their documents. Before opening a document see whether it carries a `gist`, an earlier reader's summary: it helps you choose, never stands in for the text, and one marked `stale` was written against an older version. After reading a long document, leave yours with `sum_up`.

A tool that takes a document's id takes its address too: `tisty://doc/…?id=…`.

Read a long document by parts: `brain` with `doc` gives its card — headings with their `at`, lines, sizes, pages, who cites it and the `print` — for a few hundred tokens, and `read_doc` then takes a `section`, a run of lines, or a budget of `chars`, about 12,000 characters at a time: a document longer than that asked for whole comes back as its outline, so ask for the parts. To see where it says something, `brain` with `doc` and `query` gives the lines that say it and those around each; a line two of them share comes once, with the earlier. Change what is there before adding: `edit_doc` changes the passage that is wrong, `write_doc` with the `print` you read at writes the body again when its shape no longer serves, `edit_table` changes a table by its rows and columns without touching its Markdown, and `append_doc` adds only where the order of entries is the point. A whole `write_doc` that keeps everything the document said, reordered or not, is a plain rewrite; one that takes out or folds what was there is a compaction, sent with `compact`, and with `person_asked` on the person's document when they asked for it, as `rules` «tidying» says. If anything was written in between, the print no longer matches and nothing is written — read again rather than retrying. When a write comes back saying something you did not expect, `restore_doc` puts the document back.

To hand a document to another place — Confluence, Jira, Notion, Linear, GitHub — `read_doc` with `as` «markdown» gives it whole in GitHub Markdown, and `left_out` says what does not travel: tell the person before they publish. Attached files stay here, and `export_doc` writes them into a folder for that place's tool to upload; diagrams stay mermaid fences, which Confluence and Jira show as code; links to documents and tasks become plain words; pens, underlines, icons and folds go as HTML tags. Each page is read on its own.

Handing a document to a person is theirs to do: Share in the window sends it through Folio, encrypted, and says so when Folio cannot be reached. Get it ready — current state first, open questions listed — and tell them it is ready; never paste it whole as the handover.

Everything you write has a place. `brain` with nothing, or `catch_up`, names the folders: put a document in the one its subject belongs to, as a task goes in its list. When none fits, ask the person, suggesting a name — «Shall I keep it in a new folder Work / Login?» — and make the folder only once they agree. A document stays outside every folder only when the person asks for that, or when it is Brainstorm, which the window keeps pinned at the top.

A document can hold pages, one level deep, for one long thing in parts — a book by chapters, a year of minutes; `page_doc` and `write_doc` with `page_of` say how. A locked document, or anything inside an archived folder, takes no writes at all, and only the person can lift either from the window.

A document told in several languages is one document per language, which the person sees as one with a switch between them, and `languages` names them when you read one. Write a new language with `write_doc` and `translation_of`, join two that exist with `tongue_doc`, and change each language in its own document: they are never merged into one body.

## What a document said before

Tisty keeps past versions of each document and task page, on each machine, never synced. Look there when the person asks what a document said or wants an earlier state back:
- `read_doc` with `versions` lists them, newest first; a task's id lists its page's.
- `read_doc` with `version` reads one, under a `notice` that it is not what it says now.
- `restore_doc` with `version` goes back to one over everything written since: do it when they ask, and tell them what it replaced. `restore_doc` without `version` undoes it.

One marked `elsewhere` was written over on another machine, which alone may keep it: tell the person which one instead of trying again.

## One living document per subject

A document is named by its subject, never by a ticket, a date or a round («v2», «second review»); the ticket is a tag and a task. It opens with what holds today, in the present tense; then the subject itself; then what is out of scope; then the decisions, one line each, dated, with the why — a replaced one is struck through with the day it fell, and the new one says which it replaces; last its history, one dated line per change worth knowing. What no longer holds moves down, never out: a decision to a struck line, a section to the history, a whole document to a mark. A page holds real content that relates to the subject without being part of it — a runbook, a contract, an annex — never a copy of the body nor a place to write what is new instead of consolidating. Before a new page, read its card from `brain`: what is already in the body or a page is updated there, what belongs to the subject goes in the body, and only what fits nowhere becomes a page, named in the body in reading order with a line saying what it holds. The line that places a page is `![Its title](tisty:doc/its-id)`, opening bang and all — without it the line is a mention and gives the page no place — with a backslash before a square bracket in the title; pages are read in the order their lines sit, and nothing else. A page of a few lines, or two pages on one thing, are merged back. That list of pages is the index; the headings are the outline, and nobody writes one by hand.

## Removing and putting away

You never delete. «Delete this document» is answered with `flag_doc`: it marks the document with the reason — a duplicate of which, moved where, replaced by what, wrong, obsolete, a scratch — and the person deletes it in one click, puts it away or takes the mark off; say plainly that it is marked and waiting for them. Several copies of one thing are marked in one call, so one click deletes them all or takes all their marks off. When the person asks to clean up, or your writing leaves a document stale, mark it even when it may be worth keeping: keeping it is the person's choice. «Clean up» is not «put away»: `archive_doc` puts a document away, or brings it back, only when the person says archive, put away or bring back. Nothing here closes, drops or erases a task: finishing one is `say_done`, and the closing stays theirs. An idea on Brainstorm is marked the same way, one by one, never the whole sheet: `rules` «ideas».

## Writing

Documents and what tasks say are kept in the Tisty language, the Markdown the person reads and writes in the window: plain Markdown plus pens like `==words=={green}`, underline `++words++`, icons `::dog orange::`, callouts, collapsible sections and cards to other documents. A note set apart in a document — «a highlighted note», info, a tip, a warning — is a callout, a quote that opens with `[!NOTE]`, `[!TIP]`, `[!IMPORTANT]`, `[!WARNING]` or `[!CAUTION]`, written in the document where it belongs, and `[!IDEA]` is a sticky note; `note` is a task's journal and never that. Plain Markdown is enough: it is turned into the Tisty language on the way in, and the answer says what changed under `turned`. A document is tagged by writing #word in its own text, for what it is about, reusing the tags `catch_up` shows: six is already a lot, and past 64 the rest are dropped. `language` lists what Tisty adds to Markdown and gives one topic at a time. When the person asks how to write something, point them to https://markdown.tisty.app (Spanish under /es/). Tisty links what you name exactly — a bare id, or a title in quotes that one thing carries — and lists it in `references`; what is doubtful, a lone word in quotes too, comes back in `maybe`. A picture's words say what it shows, never its file name.

## The diary

The diary is the person's own: a book a year with `diary: on` in its properties, a page a month and a section a day, today on top. It is out of your sight until the person opens it to agents from the window; while it is shut, no tool finds it. Open, you may read it, and add to it only when they ask: `append_doc` on the diary, or any of its pages, puts your words at the end of today's section, made if it is missing, under a line with the minute and your name. Nothing else changes it: no `under`, no edit, no rewrite, no move, no mark, no archive. Never start a diary of your own: `diary: on` is the person's to write.

## Assistants' files in AGENTS

A document whose properties hold an `agent:` line is an assistant's own file kept in the folder AGENTS — a rule file such as `CLAUDE.md`, a skill, a script — and Tisty writes its text, byte for byte, to the file that assistant reads, on each computer where the person turned that on. When the person asks to add or change a rule or a skill, yours included, change its document with `edit_doc`, never the file on disk: an edit made there is marked as changed outside and waits for the person. Keep it plain Markdown, since the assistant reads it as it is, and a script's document one code block, kept whole. Never write, change or take out an `agent:` line: where a file goes is the person's to decide, and a whole `write_doc` sends it back as it was. Move, merge, compact or mark these documents only when the person asks: each is one file. A memory document is the other way round: the assistant keeps writing its own memory file, and Tisty only shows it, so leave that document alone.
